## 3.2 Telemetry storage (design for free-tier quotas)

Telemetry is high volume, so do NOT write one database document per message.

- Keep an in-memory `LiveState` map per device: `lastSeenAt`, `connectivity`, `latestMetrics`, `latestHealth`. Rebuild it from the database at server start. Dashboards read live values from `LiveState` through lightweight endpoints, not from the database.
- Buffer incoming samples in memory per device. Every 60 seconds a flusher writes **one document per device per 10-minute bucket**: collection `telemetryBuckets`, document id `{deviceId}_{yyyyMMddHHmm of bucket start}`, fields `tenantId, deviceId, bucketStart, samples[] { ts, metrics{}, health{} }`. Merge the new samples into the bucket document in a single write.
- Persist `lastSeenAt` on the device document at most once every 60 seconds. Write the device document immediately only on state, connectivity, firmware, or config changes.
- History endpoints read the buckets for the requested range and down-sample on the server to at most 300 points (average per window).
- A daily retention job deletes buckets older than `TELEMETRY_RETENTION_DAYS` (default 90).

