# 5. STATE MACHINES AND ENUM RULES

## 5.1 Device lifecycle (FR-LCM-01)

States: `PROVISIONED, ACTIVE, UNDER_MAINTENANCE, SUSPENDED, DECOMMISSIONED`.

| From | To | Trigger | Who |
|---|---|---|---|
| (new) | PROVISIONED | Registration | CA, SE |
| PROVISIONED | ACTIVE | First successful device handshake | System |
| ACTIVE | UNDER_MAINTENANCE | Work order started, or manual | System, SE, CA |
| UNDER_MAINTENANCE | ACTIVE | Work order completed, or manual | System, SE, CA |
| ACTIVE | SUSPENDED | Suspend (reason required) | SE, CA |
| SUSPENDED | ACTIVE | Resume | SE, CA |
| PROVISIONED, ACTIVE, UNDER_MAINTENANCE, SUSPENDED | DECOMMISSIONED | Decommission (reason required, confirmation dialog) | CA only |
| DECOMMISSIONED | (none) | Terminal state | |

Any other transition returns 409 with the allowed transitions. Every change writes a `deviceStateHistory` row and an audit entry. Decommissioning revokes the API key (clear `apiKeyHash`), cancels queued rollout jobs and pending commands, and keeps all history read-only.

## 5.2 Connectivity (FR-MON-01)

Only two connectivity states are used: `ONLINE` and `OFFLINE` (plus `UNKNOWN` before the first heartbeat). A device is OFFLINE when no heartbeat or telemetry arrived for `offlineAfterMissedIntervals x heartbeatIntervalSec` seconds (default 3 x 30 = 90 s). Health problems (for example high CPU) are NOT a connectivity state; they are raised through alert rules.

## 5.3 Other status flows

- **Alert:** OPEN, then ACKNOWLEDGED, then CLOSED. An alert stays open until it is acknowledged and the linked ticket is closed (or an SE closes it manually with a reason). It does NOT auto-close when the metric returns to normal.
- **Ticket:** OPEN, IN_PROGRESS, WAITING_ON_CLIENT, RESOLVED, CLOSED. RESOLVED requires `rootCause` and `resolutionNotes`. A client user (CO or CA) can Confirm (RESOLVED to CLOSED) or Reopen (RESOLVED to IN_PROGRESS). An SE may close a RESOLVED ticket directly if the client does not respond (audited with reason).
- **Rollout:** PENDING_APPROVAL (only when `requireRolloutApproval` is true), PENDING, RUNNING, COMPLETED, REJECTED, CANCELLED. COMPLETED when all jobs are terminal (VERIFIED, FAILED, or CANCELLED).
- **Rollout job:** QUEUED, DOWNLOADING, INSTALLING, VERIFIED, FAILED, CANCELLED. There is no Expired state: a job for an offline device simply stays QUEUED until the device polls or the owner cancels it.
- **Firmware package:** DRAFT, RELEASED, WITHDRAWN. Only RELEASED packages can be rolled out.
- **Work order:** PLANNED, ASSIGNED, IN_PROGRESS, COMPLETED, CANCELLED. If `downtimeRequired` and the tenant has `requireMaintenanceApproval`, `approvalStatus` starts as PENDING and an FT cannot start the work order until it is APPROVED. If a CM rejects, the SE must change the window or details, which resets approval to PENDING.

# 6. DEVICE API (`/device/v1`) - used by real devices and by the simulator

Authentication on every endpoint: headers `X-Device-Id: <deviceId>` and `X-API-Key: <apiKey>`. The server hashes the key with SHA-256 and compares it in constant time to `apiKeyHash`. API keys look like `omk_` + 32 random bytes in base64url and are shown to the user exactly once.

Rejections: unknown device or wrong key returns **401**; device `SUSPENDED` or `DECOMMISSIONED`, or tenant `SUSPENDED`, returns **403**; invalid payload returns **400** with `{ error, details[] }`. Count invalid payloads per device (shown on device detail). Write a rate-limited `DEVICE_AUTH_FAILED` audit entry (at most one per device ID per 5 minutes) for failed authentications.

| Method and path | Purpose | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /handshake` | First connection and reconnection | Body `{ firmwareVersion }`. If state is PROVISIONED set ACTIVE, record history and audit `DEVICE_ACTIVATED`. Set connectivity ONLINE. Return `{ serverTime, heartbeatIntervalSec, config: { version, payload } or null }`. If a config profile is assigned and not yet applied, include it. |
| `POST /heartbeat` | Liveness and health | Body `{ timestamp, health { cpuLoad, memoryUsage, batteryLevel (nullable for mains powered), signalStrength, uptimeSec } }`. Updates `lastSeenAt` and `latestHealth`. Returns 202. |
| `POST /telemetry` | Sensor readings | Body `{ timestamp, metrics {...}, health {...} (optional) }`. Validate every metric key and type against the device model's `metrics[]`; unknown keys, wrong types, or values outside `min..max` are rejected with 400. Ignore exact duplicates (same device and timestamp). Update `LiveState`, buffer the sample, run alert rule evaluation (8.4). Returns 202. |
| `GET /commands` | Poll for work | Returns `{ config: { version, payload } or null, update: { jobId, version, downloadPath, sha256 } or null, commands: [ { id, type: "RESTART" } ] }`. Delivering an update marks the job intent as delivered but the job status only changes when the device reports it. Also counts as a heartbeat (updates `lastSeenAt`). |
| `POST /config-ack` | Configuration result | Body `{ version, status APPLIED|FAILED, error? }`. Updates `config.status`, audits `CONFIG_APPLIED` or `CONFIG_FAILED`. |
| `POST /updates/:jobId/status` | OTA progress | Body `{ status DOWNLOADING|INSTALLING|VERIFIED|FAILED, newVersion?, errorCode? }`. Rules in 8.6. |
| `GET /firmware/:firmwareId/download` | Firmware binary | Authenticated with the same device headers. Only allowed if the device has an active job for that firmware. Returns the binary and header `X-Checksum-SHA256`. There are no signed URLs. |
| `POST /commands/:id/ack` | Command result | Body `{ status ACKED|FAILED }`. |

The telemetry endpoint must respond quickly. Alert evaluation, notifications, and emails run after the response is sent, so a slow mail server can never slow down ingestion.

