# 11. DESIGN SYSTEM

- **Look:** modern enterprise dashboard: clean, calm, data-dense but readable. Neutral slate surfaces (`slate-50` page, white cards, `slate-900` sidebar), indigo primary (`indigo-600`), 12 px rounded cards with subtle borders (no heavy shadows), Inter font, 14 px base text.
- **Status colors (use everywhere, with text labels, never color alone):** Online/Active/Verified/Applied/Released/Completed = emerald; Offline/Failed/Critical/Rejected = red; Warning/Pending/Under Maintenance/Waiting on Client = amber; Provisioned/Queued/Draft/Open = sky; Suspended/Cancelled/Decommissioned/Withdrawn/Closed = slate.
- **Components:** build a small reusable library first: Button, IconButton, Input, Select, MultiSelect, Textarea, Modal, Drawer, ConfirmDialog, Tabs, DataTable (server pagination, sort, filters, empty state, loading skeleton), StatusChip, SeverityChip, KpiCard, ChartCard, Toast, Tooltip, Stepper, JsonEditor (textarea with validation), FileDrop, RelativeTime (shows absolute time on hover), EmptyState, PageHeader.
- **Behavior rules:** every list has loading skeletons, an empty state with a call to action, and an error state with Retry. Every destructive action (suspend tenant, decommission, cancel rollout, delete rule) uses ConfirmDialog; decommission requires typing the device serial number. Every form shows inline validation messages and disables Submit while saving. Success and error toasts for every mutation. Keep filter and page state in the URL query string.
- **Accessibility:** labels on all inputs, visible focus rings, color contrast AA, keyboard-operable dialogs (Escape closes, focus trap), `aria-live` for toasts, charts have text summaries.
- **Date and number formats:** `21 Nov 2026, 14:05` in local time; relative time for recent items; metric values with units and sensible rounding.
- **Responsive:** desktop first for admin pages, mobile first for `/my-work-orders`, `/work-orders/:id`, `/live-status`, and the login page.

# 12. DEVICE SIMULATOR (replaces real hardware; must use the real device API)

## 12.1 In-app simulator page (`/simulator`)

Controls for PA, SE, and CA (scoped to the working tenant):

1. **Create demo devices:** button "Create N demo devices" (N from 1 to 50, model and site selectors). It registers devices through the normal registration service, keeps their API keys in a server-only `simulatorCredentials` store, and attaches them to the simulator.
2. **Attach a registered device:** paste a provisioning file (or choose the JSON file) from the registration modal. The simulator then behaves as that device. This demonstrates UC-04 end to end.
3. **Run controls:** Start all, Stop all, per-device Start/Stop; telemetry interval (5 to 60 s, default 10 s); behavior profile (Normal, Drifting temperature, Battery drain, Random dropouts).
4. **Fault injection (per device):** Force offline (stop sending for 3 minutes), Spike a metric (choose metric and value for 2 minutes), Set low battery, Fail next OTA with CHECKSUM_MISMATCH, Fail next OTA with BOOT_FAILURE (reports ROLLED_BACK), Ignore next config.
5. **Live log:** the last 100 simulator events (handshake, heartbeats, commands, OTA steps) with timestamps.

## 12.2 Behavior of each simulated device

- On start: `POST /device/v1/handshake`, apply any returned config and `POST /config-ack` (APPLIED, unless "Ignore next config" is set, then FAILED).
- Every interval: `POST /device/v1/telemetry` with values generated per model and behavior profile (smooth random walk inside each metric's min and max, small noise) plus health values; `POST /heartbeat` at the tenant heartbeat interval; `GET /device/v1/commands` every interval.
- **Commands:** RESTART: go silent for 10 s, then re-handshake with a higher uptime reset, ack the command. APPLY_CONFIG: apply and ack.
- **OTA update:** on receiving an update: report DOWNLOADING, download the binary with `GET /device/v1/firmware/:id/download`, compute SHA-256 and compare with `X-Checksum-SHA256` and with the command's `sha256`; wait 5 s and report INSTALLING; wait 5 s and report VERIFIED with `newVersion`, then re-handshake with the new firmware version. On mismatch or injected failure report FAILED with the matching `errorCode` and keep the previous version.
- The simulator calls the device API over HTTP on `localhost`, exactly like an external device would, so every device-API rule (auth, validation, 401/403/400) is exercised.
- Server-side timers keep running only while the server process is awake; show a note about this on the page.

## 12.3 Standalone CLI (`/simulator/simulator.mjs`)

A small Node script (no dependencies beyond `node-fetch` or the built-in `fetch`) that reads `BASE_URL`, `DEVICE_ID`, `API_KEY`, `INTERVAL_SEC` from environment variables or a provisioning file path argument, and implements the same behavior as 12.2 for one device. Document it in the README so the same API can be shown from a laptop terminal.

# 13. REPORTS AND KPI DEFINITIONS (FR-RPT-01 to FR-RPT-04)

Filters on every report: date range (default last 7 days), site, device model. Use `connectivityEvents`, `alerts`, `tickets`, `workOrders`, `devices`, and `firmwarePackages`.

- **Availability %** (per device): ONLINE time divided by the period during which the device was in state ACTIVE or UNDER_MAINTENANCE within the range (computed from `connectivityEvents` and `deviceStateHistory`). Fleet availability is the mean across devices. Show per site and per model.
- **MTTR:** mean of `resolvedAt - createdAt` of tickets resolved in the range, in hours.
- **Alerts by severity:** count of alerts created in the range, per severity, plus a daily trend line.
- **Open tickets:** count with status not CLOSED at the end of the range, by priority.
- **Firmware compliance %:** devices running the latest RELEASED version of their model, divided by devices whose model has at least one RELEASED version. List non-compliant devices.
- **Maintenance completion rate:** COMPLETED work orders divided by work orders whose `plannedStart` is in the range (excluding CANCELLED).
- **Platform health (PA):** tenants, devices by state, messages per minute (last hour), invalid-payload rate, open Critical alerts, email failures.
- **Export:** CSV generated in the browser from the on-screen data (one CSV per report table, UTF-8 with header row, filename includes tenant code and date range). PDF generated in the browser with `jspdf` + `jspdf-autotable` (title, filters, KPI cards as a table, detail tables) or a print-optimized stylesheet if that fails. Exports are audited (`REPORT_EXPORTED`).

# 14. QUALITY BAR

- No unhandled promise rejections; every async route uses a wrapper that forwards errors to the error handler.
- Never return more than 200 items per request; every list is paginated.
- Avoid N+1 reads: batch lookups for names (users, devices, sites) and cache small reference data in memory with short TTL.
- Every API response shape is typed and shared between client and server via a `shared` types file.
- Log with a small structured logger (level, time, requestId). Do not log request bodies for auth routes.
- Handle server restarts gracefully: jobs resume, `LiveState` rebuilds, simulator devices stay registered but simulation stops.

